using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace Unity.Robotics
{
    // Which printed ArUco dictionary the calibration tag came from. These are OpenCV's
    // predefined dictionaries, so a tag generated by cv2.aruco, by aruco_ros, or by one of the
    // online marker generators decodes here without conversion.
    //
    // Prefer a dictionary with FEW markers for calibration: 4x4_50 tolerates one wrong bit,
    // 4x4_250 packs 250 codes into the same 16 bits and so tolerates the same single error
    // across five times as many neighbours. A bigger grid (6x6_250) is the robust choice when
    // the tag can be printed large enough to resolve 36 cells across the room.
    public enum ArucoDictionaryName
    {
        // 50 markers of 4x4 bits, corrects up to 1 wrong bit(s)
        Dict4x4_50,
        // 250 markers of 4x4 bits, corrects up to 1 wrong bit(s)
        Dict4x4_250,
        // 250 markers of 5x5 bits, corrects up to 2 wrong bit(s)
        Dict5x5_250,
        // 250 markers of 6x6 bits, corrects up to 5 wrong bit(s)
        Dict6x6_250,
    }

    // The marker code tables for the supported OpenCV dictionaries, plus the lookup that turns
    // an observed grid of bits back into a marker id and orientation.
    //
    // The tables below are DATA, not something derivable: OpenCV's predefined dictionaries are
    // pseudo-random code sets chosen to maximise inter-marker Hamming distance, so there is no
    // formula to regenerate them from. They were dumped from opencv-contrib 5.0.0 and every
    // entry was verified against what cv2.aruco.generateImageMarker actually draws for that id -
    // which is the check that matters, because the printed tag is the ground truth, not the byte
    // table. Regenerating them by hand is not a thing to attempt; a single wrong entry shows up
    // only as a tag that never decodes.
    //
    // Bit convention: row-major, MSB first, starting at the marker's top-left cell as printed.
    // A 1 bit is a WHITE cell (which is how OpenCV renders them). The black quiet border around
    // the grid is not part of the code - it is what the detector uses to reject candidates.
